# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EOL policy: LF in the index, LF on disk, on every platform.
#
# Why this file exists (the byte-artifact integrity story):
# This repo commits a prebuilt `dist/` and ships it by GitHub TAG tarball —
# server/hub/windows/tizen consume exactly those bytes, and `scripts/
# check-dist-clean.mjs` (S176) md5-audits the whole tracked dist tree after a
# clean build. `src/api/test/server-route-manifest.json` is vendored verbatim
# from phlix-contracts and byte-identity-pinned by md5 in `src/api/test/
# routeGate.api.test.ts`. Git-for-Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so a
# windows-latest checkout would silently rewrite every LF text blob (dist
# bundles, declarations, the vendored manifest) to CRLF on disk before those
# hashes are taken — the exact poisoning class fixed on the windows client by
# PR #46 / commit 0c6163d. The committed blobs are all LF; checkout conversion
# is the only threat.
#
# .gitattributes settings take precedence over core.autocrlf, so pinning the
# policy here makes the shipped and vendored bytes identical on disk in every
# clone, dev machine, and CI shard — and keeps every md5/SHA pin strict
# (byte-exact) instead of forcing it to tolerate CRLF.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Treat files as text by content: normalize to LF on commit, check out as LF.
# Covers src/**, dist/** (committed build output), docs/**, *.md, JSON,
# lockfiles, YAML, scripts — the whole estate-standard text surface.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never EOL-normalized on add or checkout (a converted font or
# poster is a corrupted one; `binary` also keeps them out of
# `git add --renormalize`'s path). Extensions from this repo's actual tracked
# inventory (src/assets fonts/images, mockup renders, demo media).
*.png binary
*.woff2 binary
*.mp4 binary
